CONTRIBUTION_TYPE: design
SCALE_REALISM: Not established. The L0 math is coherent, and the reported p* trend supports a design-level gate: long streamed results make required P(correct|fire) low. But the artifact uses synthetic front/tail distributions, assumed detector accuracy, and simulated rho/W. It does not measure real tool-result corpora, real partial-stream detectability, scheduler costs, growing-prefix prefill costs, side-effect reversibility, cancellation/rollback semantics, or production workload distributions. Reproducibility is acceptable for the L0 artifact as described (`sim_early_commit.py`, CSVs, 7 seeds, stdlib), but not for the claimed real workload effect.
RATIONALE: I found no shipping OSS system among vLLM/SGLang/LMCache/FlashInfer/TensorRT-LLM, plus common agent streaming frameworks, that already implements the specific mechanism: commit the next reasoning step from a partial streamed tool result and use a correctness-safety break-even over detectability, rho, and redo cost. Existing systems cover adjacent pieces: streaming outputs, speculative token decoding, prefix/KV reuse, structured/tool parsing, and agent event streaming. Therefore this is more than integration-only, but it remains yellow because the load-bearing empirical premises in the packet are explicitly unmeasured: real front-loading rate, real detector P(correct|fire), real rho, redo cost W, and reversibility. Prompt version: v001.
